# Released ptah binaries read the advisory snapshots below live from master
# (internal/advisorysource.DefaultLocation), so a change merged here reaches
# every installed binary without a release. The rules below notify the
# repository owner of each change to the snapshots, to the code that fetches,
# validates and merges them, to the workflow that proposes a refresh, and to
# this file. They are a notification, not a protection: GitHub enforces
# nothing here unless branch protection requires code-owner review, and an
# author cannot approve their own pull request. What protects a scan is the
# rule the binary applies, vulnerability.EvaluateWithFloor for PostgreSQL and
# vulnerability.EvaluateVersionedWithFloor for the other engines: each
# advisory is matched against a copy read from master and against the
# snapshot built into the binary, and the stricter answer is reported, so a
# copy can add a finding but cannot make any advisory's answer cleaner than
# the built-in snapshot's. A copy can still let a run pass that the built-in
# snapshot alone fails. A copy retrieved later renews freshness, for at most
# 30 days from a retrieval time up to 24 hours ahead of the scanning machine's
# clock. An advisory the built-in snapshot reads as uncertain and the copy as
# affected loses its coverage gap, so --fail-on incomplete, a suppression or
# --ignore can pass. A change to the merging or validating code below reaches
# a binary only in a release.

/internal/vulnerability/*.json @denisvmedia
/internal/advisorysource/ @denisvmedia
/internal/vulnerability/merge.go @denisvmedia
/internal/vulnerability/origin.go @denisvmedia
/internal/vulnerability/snapshot.go @denisvmedia
/internal/vulnerability/versioned.go @denisvmedia
/.github/workflows/security-snapshots.yml @denisvmedia
/.github/CODEOWNERS @denisvmedia
